00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:37.229
When you hear the phrase “brain-eating amoebas,”

00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:39.858
is there a particular image that comes to mind?

00:00:39.858 --> 00:00:45.320
Maybe you're conjuring up a malignant blob, 
one that creeps and eats and

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:51.710
creeps some more, its size growing with every
chunk it rips out of your brain.

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:57.510
Or maybe you're painting a picture of a
shape-shifting pacman-like organism bent on

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:58.881
terrorizing your body.

00:00:58.881 --> 00:01:04.129
Or maybe there’s nothing, just, you know, the 
existential dread that happens when you mash

00:01:04.129 --> 00:01:10.094
a bunch of seemingly unrelated words together, 
like “brain-eating” and “amoeba,” only to realize the

00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:12.930
threat is actually pretty real.

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:18.030
Whatever you envision (or do not), would
it be strange to suggest that the notorious

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:23.473
brain-eating amoeba that strikes fear in our
hearts actually looks something like

00:01:23.473 --> 00:01:25.190
these little guys?

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:29.895
Like tiny little bits of fluff that have just
stumbled into your path?

00:01:29.895 --> 00:01:33.687
Now to be very clear, these are not 
brain-eating amoebas.

00:01:33.687 --> 00:01:35.180
At least, not as far as we know.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:40.419
They belong to the same general group of organisms
though called amoeboflagellates.

00:01:40.419 --> 00:01:44.370
And since we do not, you know, have 
some brain-eating microbes on hand,

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:48.350
we are going to use these 
amoeboflagellates as sort of a proxy.

00:01:48.350 --> 00:01:53.790
As you might guess from the name,
amoeboflagellates take all the crawling and shape-shifting

00:01:53.790 --> 00:02:00.795
blobbiness that we love from amoebas, and
combine it with the flagella that powers flagellates.

00:02:00.795 --> 00:02:04.650
It’s actually a little more complicated than that,
and that is why we are focusing

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.620
on brain-eating amoebas today.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:13.672
Because they have actually been very well-studied, 
because they impact human life pretty substantially.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:20.459
In 1961, a 9 year old boy died in South Australia.

00:02:20.459 --> 00:02:24.111
Four years later, two eight year old girls
died as well.

00:02:24.111 --> 00:02:29.400
They had been healthy, but four days before
their death, they reported feeling tired.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.537
The next day, they all had painful headaches
along with a fever and other symptoms.

00:02:34.537 --> 00:02:39.000
Around the same time, a twenty-eight year
old man arrived at the hospital with similar

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:42.084
symptoms, and died shortly after.

00:02:42.084 --> 00:02:48.540
These cases were documented by Doctor M. Fowler,
and they were all diagnosed with meningitis,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.790
an inflammation of the membranes that protect
the brain and spinal cord.

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:56.540
Meningitis can be caused by bacterial or viral
infections.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:01.030
But when doctors studied the bodies of the
patients after their death, they did not

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:04.277
find any bacteria or viruses.

00:03:04.277 --> 00:03:06.343
They found amoeba.

00:03:06.913 --> 00:03:09.033
This was a surprise.

00:03:09.033 --> 00:03:13.837
There are other amoebas that dig their way
into human tissues, like Entamoeba histolytica,

00:03:13.837 --> 00:03:18.673
the parasite behind diseases like amebiasis
and amebic colitis.

00:03:18.673 --> 00:03:25.090
But while Entamoeba histolytica can lead to
meningitis, it is an intestinal parasite.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:29.135
The organism that killed these patients was
something entirely different:

00:03:29.135 --> 00:03:34.005
a free-living organism, not a parasite, 
that just happened to find itself in a

00:03:34.005 --> 00:03:39.135
prime position in a human nasal cavity to kill.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:46.048
The amoeba would later be named Naegleria
fowleri for the doctor who first described it.

00:03:46.048 --> 00:03:52.010
And it is very important to understand that
Naegleria fowleri does not rely on our brains

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.486
to survive.

00:03:53.486 --> 00:03:59.220
Members of the Naegleria genus are found around
the world in both soil and water.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:03.879
They swim around in lakes and non-chlorinated
pools and hot springs.

00:04:03.879 --> 00:04:09.340
They can even be found in the nasal passages
of humans who are entirely healthy.

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:14.379
Ecologically, these amoeba likely play an
important role in making sure bacteria populations

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:16.669
don’t go out of control by eating them.

00:04:16.669 --> 00:04:21.650
And some, like Naegleria fowleri, love hot
water, which is why they tend to proliferate

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.000
in the summer.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.910
Naegleria also change up their form depending
on the conditions around them.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:32.739
Normally, they exist in their amoeboid form
called a trophozoite.

00:04:32.739 --> 00:04:38.520
But when food is scarce or conditions become
unfriendly, they might transition towards

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.100
their flagellated state.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.000
And if things get very, very bad, then Naegleria
pull one of the most popular moves in the

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:47.260
microcosmos:

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:53.330
they ball up into a protective cyst, putting
themselves on pause until things improve and

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.686
they feel ready to emerge.

00:04:55.686 --> 00:05:02.240
Imagine Naegleria spending their days swimming
around, looking for bacteria, and curling up into cysts

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.602
when they feel the need for protection…

00:05:04.602 --> 00:05:08.140
it’s all a bit quaint for a killer amoeba.

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:13.470
But perhaps that is Naegleria fowleri’s
greatest transformation, the one it embarks

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:17.470
on when it goes from water to a human body.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:24.020
That shift starts when it inadvertently finds
itself inhaled into a nasal cavity, usually

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.750
by someone who has been playing around or
swimming in infected water.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:33.870
The nose might not be where the amoeba is intended
to be, but assigning intention in the microcosmos

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:35.669
is a pointless endeavor.

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:41.906
The amoeba has found itself in a new habitat,
and so it must adapt.

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:47.745
It begins by sticking itself to the mucus-y
surfaces around it.

00:05:47.745 --> 00:05:50.824
The body does not welcome Naegleria fowleri.

00:05:50.824 --> 00:05:56.010
Within six hours, the immune system will attempt
to mount one of its first lines of defense,

00:05:56.010 --> 00:06:01.039
using a type of white blood cells called neutrophils
to try and control the amoeba.

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:06.720
And over the next few days, the amoeba will
continue its path, undeterred by our body’s

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.180
defenses as it reaches the olfactory bulb.

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:15.490
And along its path, Naegleria fowleri will
release enzymes that break down the olfactory

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:22.789
epithelium it passes by, causing destruction
that is felt by the human whose body has become

00:06:22.789 --> 00:06:25.657
an unwitting home to the amoeba.

00:06:26.929 --> 00:06:32.168
Now we keep calling this a case of brain-eating
amoeba, but the disease itself is actually called

00:06:32.168 --> 00:06:35.107
primary amoebic meningoencephalitis.

00:06:35.107 --> 00:06:40.470
And as we described in the first reported
cases, the symptoms are painful.

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:44.387
Headaches, vomiting, fatigue, even a coma.

00:06:44.387 --> 00:06:48.810
Patients usually die within a week of
these symptoms.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.175
Now the good news is that these 
infections are not common.

00:06:52.175 --> 00:06:58.340
Between 1962 and 2018, there were 45 cases
in the US, the country that has

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:00.590
reported the most cases.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:05.937
Of course this only accounts for infections
that were reported, so there may be more.

00:07:05.937 --> 00:07:12.902
The bad news is that survival is exceedingly
rare, only around 5% thanks to a drug called

00:07:12.902 --> 00:07:14.591
amphotericin.

00:07:14.591 --> 00:07:18.639
But if a treatment exists, then 
why is survival so rare?

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:24.910
Remember that amoebas are not the only organisms
that cause meningitis, so narrowing down on

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.597
Naegleria fowleri as the culprit can be difficult.

00:07:28.597 --> 00:07:34.480
And given the rapid destruction of the amoeba,
a delayed diagnosis can mean death.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.396
So what do we do with that?

00:07:36.396 --> 00:07:41.470
What do we do with the knowledge that out
there in a lake somewhere is an amoeboflagellate

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:47.633
capable, with the release of a few proteins
and a trip down our nose, of killing us?

00:07:49.273 --> 00:07:52.665
I guess we do what we always do.

00:07:52.665 --> 00:07:59.793
We watch and learn and try to know more about
a vast world hiding around us.

00:07:59.793 --> 00:08:05.729
A world constantly undergoing tiny little
transitions between friend and foe.

00:08:05.729 --> 00:08:11.900
Or perhaps, more accurately, a world where
friend and foe is a dichotomy too harsh to

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:16.418
grasp int the myriad relationships in between.

00:08:20.215 --> 00:08:26.161
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as 
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

